.SH "NAME"
pcre2_substitute \- replace matched substrings
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
\f(CR\&#include <pcre2.h>

int\fP \f(CBpcre2_substitute\fP\f(CR(const pcre2_code *\fP\f(CIcode\fP\f(CR, PCRE2_SPTR\fP \f(CIsubject\fP\f(CR,
                     PCRE2_SIZE\fP \f(CIlength\fP\f(CR, PCRE2_SIZE\fP \f(CIstart_offset\fP\f(CR,
                     uint32_t\fP \f(CIoptions\fP\f(CR, pcre2_match_data *\fP\f(CImatch_data\fP\f(CR,
                     pcre2_match_context *\fP\f(CImcontext\fP\f(CR,
                     PCRE2_SPTR\fP \f(CIreplacement\fP\f(CR, PCRE2_SIZE\fP \f(CIrlength\fP\f(CR,
                     PCRE2_UCHAR *\fP\f(CIbuffer\fP\f(CR, PCRE2_SIZE *\fP\f(CIblength\fP\f(CR);\fP
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
This function matches a compiled regular expression against a given subject
string, using a matching algorithm that is similar to Perl\(cqs. It then makes a
copy of the subject, substituting a replacement string for what was matched.
Its arguments are:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 13
\f(CIcode\fP
.ad l
Points to the compiled pattern
.TP 13
\f(CIsubject\fP
.ad l
Points to the subject string
.TP 13
\f(CIlength\fP
.ad l
Length of the subject string
.TP 13
\f(CIstart_offset\fP
.ad l
Offset in the subject at which to start matching
.TP 13
\f(CIoptions\fP
.ad l
Option bits
.TP 13
\f(CImatch_data\fP
.ad l
Points to a match data block, or is \f(CBNULL\fP
.TP 13
\f(CImcontext\fP
.ad l
Points to a match context, or is \f(CBNULL\fP
.TP 13
\f(CIreplacement\fP
.ad l
Points to the replacement string
.TP 13
\f(CIrlength\fP
.ad l
Length of the replacement string
.TP 13
\f(CIbuffer\fP
.ad l
Points to the output buffer, or may be \f(CBNULL\fP if \f(CIblength\fP is zero
.TP 13
\f(CIblength\fP
.ad l
Points to the length of the output buffer
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
A match data block is needed only if you want to inspect the data from the
final match that is returned in that block or if \f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_MATCHED\fP is
set. A match context is needed only if you want to:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Set up a callout function
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Set a matching offset limit
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Change the backtracking match limit
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Change the backtracking depth limit
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Set custom memory management in the match context
.RE
.sp
The \f(CIlength\fP, \f(CIstart_offset\fP and \f(CIrlength\fP values are code units,
not characters, as is the contents of the variable pointed at by
\f(CIblength\fP. This variable must contain the length of the output buffer
when the function is called. If the function is successful, the value is
changed to the length of the new string, excluding the trailing zero that is
automatically added.
.sp
The subject and replacement lengths can be given as \f(CBPCRE2_ZERO_TERMINATED\fP for
zero\-terminated strings. The options are:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_ANCHORED\fP
.ad l
Match only at the first position
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_ENDANCHORED\fP
.ad l
Match only at end of subject
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_NOTBOL\fP
.ad l
Subject is not the beginning of a
line
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_NOTEOL\fP
.ad l
Subject is not the end of a line
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_NOTEMPTY\fP
.ad l
An empty string is not a
valid match
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_NOTEMPTY_ATSTART\fP
.ad l
An empty string at the start of
the subject is not a valid match
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_NO_JIT\fP
.ad l
Do not use JIT matching
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_NO_UTF_CHECK\fP
.ad l
Do not check for UTF validity in
the subject or replacement
(only relevant if \f(CBPCRE2_UTF\fP was
set at compile time)
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_EXTENDED\fP
.ad l
Do extended replacement processing
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_GLOBAL\fP
.ad l
Replace all occurrences in the
subject
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_LITERAL\fP
.ad l
The replacement string is literal
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_MATCHED\fP
.ad l
Use pre\-existing match data for
first match
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_OVERFLOW_LENGTH\fP
.ad l
If overflow, compute needed length
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_REPLACEMENT_ONLY\fP
.ad l
Return only replacement string(s)
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_UNKNOWN_UNSET\fP
.ad l
Treat unknown group as unset
.TP 26
\f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_UNSET_EMPTY\fP
.ad l
Simple unset insert = empty string
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
If \f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_LITERAL\fP is set, \f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_EXTENDED\fP,
\f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_UNKNOWN_UNSET\fP, and \f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_UNSET_EMPTY\fP are ignored.
.sp
If \f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_MATCHED\fP is set, \f(CImatch_data\fP must be non\-\f(CBNULL\fP; its
contents must be the result of a call to \f(CBpcre2_match()\fP (or
\f(CBpcre2_jit_match()\fP) using the same pattern, subject pointer, effective
subject length, start offset, and match options.
.sp
The function returns the number of substitutions, which may be zero if there
are no matches. The result may be greater than one only when
\f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_GLOBAL\fP is set. In the event of an error, a negative error code
is returned.
.sp
There is a complete description of the PCRE2 native API in the
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
page and a description of the POSIX API in the
\f(CRpcre2posix\fP
page.